How does digital EHS connect the control room and the field?
Digital EHS connects power plant control-room and field teams through shared, time-stamped records of permits, inspections, observations and actions. It helps authorised personnel communicate changing conditions, assign responsibility and track follow-up. Effective implementation also requires clear acknowledgement, visible data freshness and established communication procedures.
The objective is a dependable flow of information between the people planning work, those carrying it out and those responsible for oversight.

Different views of the same working environment
The control room provides visibility into plant operating conditions. Field personnel add information that may not be apparent on a process display: an obstructed access route, an unexpected work-area condition or a contractor activity nearby.

Maintenance supervisors bring another perspective, including task requirements and equipment history. EHS teams help review hazards, controls and outstanding concerns.

These views become more useful when they share a common reference.

A report stating “issue near the pump” leaves room for confusion. A record linked to the correct equipment identifier, location, activity and observation time gives the recipient a clearer starting point.

The UK Health and Safety Executive’s guidance on safety-critical communication highlights the importance of identifying who needs information, selecting suitable communication methods and providing information at the right time.

Five connections that matter in daily operations
1. Connect work authorisation with current field conditions
A permit records the conditions and authorisations associated with a defined activity. Its usefulness depends on people understanding its scope and current status.

Digital workflows can make it easier to see whether work is awaiting review, authorised, suspended or completed, where those states are configured.

When field conditions change, the relevant information needs to reach the responsible personnel through the site’s agreed process. A previously approved record should never be treated as proof that conditions remain unchanged.

