A practical example: a condition changes before maintenance
The following is an illustrative workflow, not a customer case study.

A field team arrives for planned maintenance and finds that temporary equipment is obstructing the agreed access route.

The team follows the site’s established communication and work-control procedures. The observation is recorded against the relevant location and activity, with the time and supporting information.

The responsible supervisor reviews the concern and coordinates with affected personnel. Any required change to the work status is handled through the authorised process.

Once the concern has been addressed, the appropriate person verifies the condition and records the result. If the matter remains open at shift change, it is included in the handover.

The value of digital EHS lies in preserving that chain of information and responsibility—from the original observation to the verified outcome.

“Sent” does not mean “understood”
A notification can be delivered without being read. A record can be opened without the recipient understanding what action is required.

For significant communications, implementation planning should define the difference between:

Recorded → delivered → acknowledged → reviewed → acted on → verified.

Not every observation needs all six stages. The response should reflect the nature and urgency of the information.

Urgent concerns must follow the plant’s established emergency and operational communication procedures. Employees should not wait for an app notification to be read when immediate communication is required.

Make information freshness visible
A dashboard can look current while displaying an older record.

Teams need to distinguish when an observation occurred, when it was entered and when it last synchronised. Those times may differ, particularly where connectivity is limited.

Where offline functions are supported, the implementation should explain which activities remain available offline and which require an online connection or direct confirmation.

An unsynchronised inspection record is not confirmation that another team has received it. Similarly, an old permit status should not be presented as a current authorisation.

These details are important when evaluating a mobile EHS system for field use.

Where AI can help
Within an appropriately configured system, AI can assist with reviewing information—for example, grouping similar observations, identifying recurring themes or drafting summaries for a person to check.

A draft handover summary could help a supervisor locate relevant open records. It should retain links to the source information and make uncertainty visible.

AI-generated summaries may omit context or interpret a report incorrectly. Responsible personnel must review them before using them for operational decisions.

The foundation remains a well-designed digital workflow. Reminders, routing rules and approval steps can be valuable without requiring AI.

Digital EHS and plant control systems have different roles
Digital EHS manages work-related information, responsibilities and evidence. Distributed control systems, SCADA and safety systems perform separate operational functions.

An EHS status must not be assumed to represent the physical state of equipment.

Where information is exchanged between systems, the integration needs a defined purpose, agreed ownership, appropriate access controls and technical review. A connection should not be assumed merely because both systems contain the same equipment name.

How to assess whether the connection is improving
Start with one workflow, such as an inspection finding that requires maintenance follow-up.

Ask field and control-room users where information becomes unclear or delayed. Then agree on the records, responsibilities and communication steps needed to improve it.

Useful measures include:

Time from reporting to review.

Priority items awaiting acknowledgement.

Open concerns carried into the next shift without a clear owner.

Actions closed with verification evidence.

Reports that recur after an earlier action was completed.

Review these measures alongside workforce feedback. A faster response is useful only if the information is understood and the response is appropriate.

Frequently asked questions
What is digital EHS for power plants?
Digital EHS is software used to manage safety and environmental workflows, including inspections, permits, observations, incidents and corrective actions. It helps teams maintain accessible records and clear responsibility across activities.

Can digital EHS replace radio communication?
No. It can support records and follow-up, but urgent and safety-critical communication must follow the plant’s established procedures. Direct communication may still be necessary.

Does digital EHS replace DCS or SCADA?
No. EHS software supports work coordination and oversight. DCS and SCADA serve plant monitoring and control functions. Any integration requires a separately defined scope.

Can field teams use digital EHS without connectivity?
Some systems support selected offline activities. The supported functions, synchronisation behaviour and restrictions must be confirmed for the implementation. Offline capture does not mean that other teams have received the information.

How can AI support shift handovers?
AI can help prepare summaries and identify related records where those capabilities are implemented. Supervisors should check the summary against the source records and confirm unresolved items with the incoming team.

What should a power plant digitise first?
Start with a workflow that has clear ownership and a visible communication problem. Inspection findings, corrective-action follow-up or permit-status visibility can provide a focused starting point.

Build a shared understanding of the work
Control-room operators and field teams each hold information the other may need.

Digital EHS helps organise that information so people can see what has changed, understand their responsibilities and follow through on outstanding work.

